{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1792483085897,"sessionID":"ses_ea7af1b22ffe4nHHVEquQPd1zB","part":{"id":"prt_158514e3d00132TVIxJAwMo5vB","messageID":"msg_158513d2d001Qd7ZYQh5Tb2ujk","sessionID":"ses_ea7af1b22ffe4nHHVEquQPd1zB","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1792483089738,"sessionID":"ses_ea7af1b22ffe4nHHVEquQPd1zB","part":{"id":"prt_158514e43001wBXZR4Jg34J7GN","messageID":"msg_158513d2d001Qd7ZYQh5Tb2ujk","sessionID":"ses_ea7af1b22ffe4nHHVEquQPd1zB","type":"text","text":"Written to `submission/main.c`.\n\nStructure: `GPIO_DT_SPEC_GET` pulls both specs from the `led0` and `sw0` aliases at compile time, with `#error` guards if a board lacks them. LED is configured `GPIO_OUTPUT_INACTIVE` (off), button is `GPIO_INPUT | GPIO_PULL_UP` with `GPIO_INT_EDGE_BOTH`, and the callback just calls `gpio_pin_toggle_dt(&led)`. `main()` ends on `k_sleep(K_FOREVER)`.\n\nTwo notes on the implementation:\n- Both-edge triggering means each press fires twice (press and release), so the LED toggles back. If you want one toggle per press, switch to `GPIO_INT_EDGE_TO_ACTIVE`.\n- There's no debounce, so mechanical switch bounce can produce extra toggles. A `k_work_delayable` or timestamp check in the callback handles that if it matters on your hardware.","time":{"start":1792483085891,"end":1792483089701}}}
